# Build-context excludes for every image in the repo.
#
# This has to live at the REPO ROOT: Docker reads `.dockerignore` from the build context root and nowhere
# else, and every Dockerfile here builds with `context: ../..` (the services need libs/, the web bundle needs
# the pnpm workspace packages). apps/web/.dockerignore was therefore never consulted — it sat next to the
# Dockerfile rather than at the context root, so node_modules went into the context regardless.
#
# Nothing below is needed to BUILD: the service images run `dotnet restore` + `dotnet publish` themselves and
# the web image runs its own `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. Host artefacts are at best 50-60 MB of context
# transferred per service, and at worst actively wrong — a stale host bin/obj is exactly the "restored source
# but kept old binaries" failure mode.
#
# It also makes the build work from a git worktree at all: pnpm lays node_modules out as symlinks pointing
# back into the main checkout, and BuildKit fails outright on them
# ("cannot replace to directory .../node_modules/.bin with file").

# Node / pnpm
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.vite
**/coverage
**/playwright-report
**/test-results

# .NET build output
**/bin
**/obj
**/TestResults

# VCS + local noise
.git
.gitignore
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store

# Never let a local secrets file reach a build context.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
# The stanza above said "never let a local secrets file reach a build context" and then listed only .env
# files, so the issuer's PEM keys were still being sent. They are mounted read-only at runtime from
# infra/compose/secrets (compose.yaml, `/run/secrets`) and no Dockerfile copies one, so nothing needs them
# here — and `docker build` from the repo root FAILED outright on them, because they are 0400 and owned by
# the container's uid, so the daemon could not read them while packing the context. A build that dies on a
# file it should never have been offered is the same bug wearing a different hat.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
infra/compose/secrets/
